Newton's Three Laws of Motion

1. First Law of Motion (Law of Inertia)

An object remains at rest or continues to move with a uniform velocity in a straight line unless acted upon by a net external, unbalanced force.

Inertia: The inherent property of a body to resist changes in its state of rest, motion, or direction.

Inertia of Rest: A person standing in a bus falls backward when the bus suddenly moves.

Inertia of Motion: A passenger falls forward when a moving bus brakes suddenly.

Inertia of Direction: Passengers shift sideways when a vehicle takes a sharp turn.

2. Second Law of Motion (Law of Force & Acceleration)

The rate of change of linear momentum of an object is directly proportional to the applied unbalanced force and takes place in the direction of the force.

Mathematical Derivation:

𝐹⃗∝𝑑𝑝⃗/𝑑𝑡⟹𝐹⃗=𝑑(𝑚𝑣⃗)/𝑑𝑡

For a system with a constant mass (𝑚):𝐹⃗=𝑚𝑑𝑣⃗/𝑑𝑡=𝑚𝑎⃗

3. Third Law of Motion (Law of Action-Reaction)

To every action, there is always an equal and opposite reaction. Forces always occur in matched interaction pairs.

Mathematical Statement: If object 𝐴 exerts a force on object B (𝐹⃗𝐴𝐵), then object 𝐵 exerts an equal and opposite force on object 𝐴(𝐹⃗𝐵𝐴).

𝐹⃗𝐴𝐵=−𝐹⃗𝐵𝐴

Key Properties: Action and reaction forces act on two different bodies, meaning they never cancel each other out dynamically.

Examples: Recoil of a fired gun, walking on the ground, or rocket propulsion.